//go:build ios

package runtime

import "internal/goarch"

// Added to Go's runtime for the iOS family at build time (cmd/build_libbox applies it through
// `go build -overlay`, beside edits to mspanset.go, proc.go, malloc.go and asm_arm64.s). The iOS
// family is the ios build tag, not GOOS: gomobile builds tvOS with GOOS=darwin and the tags
// ios,tvos. A packet tunnel there lives under a 50 MiB jetsam limit with a Go heap of a few MiB,
// and three of the runtime's choices for large servers cost it more than the heap they serve.

// The GC keeps its spans in spanSets: four per size class, each holding its spans in blocks of
// 512 pointers (4 KiB) indexed by a spine of 256 more (2 KiB), allocated off-heap and never
// returned. A heap of a few MiB uses about a hundred size classes and a handful of spans in each,
// so those blocks came to 1.8 MiB of the tunnel's resident memory against 0.1 MiB of spans. Blocks
// of 32 hold the same spans in 256 bytes; a large heap only grows the spine more often.
const (
	clashSpanSetBlockEntries = 32
	clashSpanSetInitSpineCap = 16
)

// Small goroutine stacks come from spans of _StackCacheSize, 32 KiB upstream: eight 4 KiB stacks to
// a span, and a span goes back to the heap only when all of them are free. A tunnel starts with
// a few hundred goroutines at once (every group's first health check), keeps sixty, and the
// survivors are spread over the spans the burst took: on the 13 mini 0.7 MiB of free stack slots
// stayed pinned. Spans of 16 KiB -- one page of the platform's -- pin half as much (its stacks
// 1.97 -> 1.55-1.61 MiB idle); stacks of 16 KiB, which no longer fit the pool, take the large-stack
// path, a page of their own.
const clashStackCacheSize = 16 * 1024

// clashAppleSmallProcess makes an exited goroutine give back its stack. Upstream keeps the stack
// of every goroutine that exits at the starting size on its P's free list, up to 63 a P, for the
// next goroutine to reuse; on iOS the starting size is 4 KiB, and after a burst of short-lived
// goroutines those stacks and the stack spans they pin stay resident until the P's list happens
// to overflow. Freed, the stack goes back through the P's stack cache like any other.
const clashAppleSmallProcess = true

// clashAppleDropExtraM gives a non-Go thread's m back after every call into Go, as the runtime
// did before Go 1.21, instead of keeping it bound to the thread until the thread exits. Swift
// calls into the core from whichever dispatch thread it is on, so bound ms -- each with a 64 KiB
// signal stack and a goroutine stack -- accumulated with the dispatch threads that ever called,
// where dropped ones number only the calls that were in Go at the same time. The cost is a needm
// and a dropm per call, which the platform's control-plane call rate does not notice.
//
// Only on arm64: asm_arm64.s reads this flag, and the amd64 simulator slices keep upstream's
// binding in both halves (needAndBindM and the cgocallback epilogue) rather than one of them.
var clashAppleDropExtraM = goarch.IsArm64 == 1

// clashAppleRuntimeOverlayMarker exists so the finished slice can prove the overlay was compiled
// in; build_libbox asks nm for it in every iOS-family slice. schedinit calls it.
//
//go:noinline
func clashAppleRuntimeOverlayMarker() {}
